// == TRACK 4 - LAYER 1 - PULSE ===============================================
// LIGHTCYCLE's drums. Relentless where track 3's open up only in the B half:
// this track's whole proposition is that it never eases off, so the 16th hats
// run for all eight bars and the fills push rather than breathe.
//
// SYNTHESISED, NOT SAMPLED — the one change that moves this track from "near
// the genre" to "in it". Every other stem here is an oscillator; the kit was
// the only sampled thing in the whole soundtrack, and `s("bd")` with no bank
// pulls Dirt-Samples, which is an acoustic/lo-fi kit. Against a Tron brief that
// reads as a live drummer sitting in a synth track.
//
// The instinct is `.bank("RolandTR909")`. It would silently do nothing:
// render_superdough.mjs hardcodes Dirt-Samples as the only default sample map
// and the TR machines live in a pack that is never loaded, so the bank control
// is accepted and ignored. Adding a --samples flag would also put a network
// fetch in the middle of a build that is otherwise offline and deterministic.
//
// So the kit is built from oscillators and noise instead, which is what a 909
// physically is — sampling one is the detour. Same vocabulary the SFX bank
// already ships (audio_src/sfx/*.strudel), so this is proven in this pipeline,
// not a new dependency. Side benefit: no sample sound is referenced anywhere in
// the stem, so the renderer skips sample loading entirely.
//
// PITCH ENVELOPES ARE POSITIVE HERE. superdough's penv OFFSETS THE START of the
// note upward and decays to the WRITTEN pitch (audio-authoring.md gotcha 4), so
// a drum — which starts high and falls — is penv(POSITIVE) with the note
// written as the pitch it lands on. Do not "fix" these to negative.
//
// The one thing kept sparse is the crash. A racing loop heard for minutes needs
// somewhere for the ear to rest, and if the kit is constant that rest has to
// come from what is ABSENT rather than from what is added.
setcps(0.5)
stack(
  // KICK — sine with a fast downward sweep: a0 + penv(26) starts near c3 and
  // falls to 27.5 Hz in 45 ms. Distortion on the kick ALONE, never on a bus:
  // it is the only element with the headroom to spend on harmonics, and those
  // harmonics are what let a 27 Hz fundamental survive a laptop speaker.
  note("a0*4").s("sine").penv(26).pdecay(0.045)
    .attack(0.001).decay(0.17).sustain(0)
    .distort("1.3:.5").gain(0.46),

  // CLAP — three noise bursts 31 ms apart, then a longer body underneath. That
  // burst train is what physically makes a clap a clap; a single burst reads as
  // a snare no matter how it is filtered. The 32nd-note nesting is doing the
  // 31 ms spacing, so the flam is on the grid rather than hand-placed.
  s("~ [[white white white ~] ~ ~ ~] ~ [[white white white ~] ~ ~ ~]")
    .hpf(1100).lpf(6800).attack(0.001).decay(0.022).sustain(0).gain(0.28),
  s("~ white ~ white")
    .hpf(1000).lpf(5800).attack(0.012).decay(0.17).sustain(0)
    .gain(0.12).room(0.22).size(0.5),

  // SNARE body under the clap, for the crack the noise cannot make on its own.
  // A tuned triangle with its own short drop — the pitched component of a snare,
  // separated out so its level can be set independently of the noise.
  note("~ d3 ~ d3").s("triangle").penv(9).pdecay(0.02)
    .attack(0.001).decay(0.085).sustain(0).gain(0.13),

  // 16th hats, every bar, all eight. This is the tyre noise. Panned narrow and
  // moving, so it sits behind the kick instead of on top of it.
  // Noise hats carry far more sustained energy than the sampled hats they
  // replaced — the whole stem measured 4.5 dB over its siblings on the first
  // render, and these 16ths were most of it.
  s("white*16").hpf(9200).attack(0.0005).decay(0.014).sustain(0)
    .gain(0.085).pan(sine.range(0.40, 0.60).fast(4)),

  // Offbeat open hat, every bar. The lift that stops four-on-the-floor from
  // reading as a metronome. Lower hpf and a long decay is the entire difference
  // between a closed hat and an open one when both are filtered noise.
  s("[~ white]*4").hpf(7600).attack(0.0005).decay(0.135).sustain(0).gain(0.11),

  // Rimshot doubling the "and" of 3 and 4 in the B half only. The one place the
  // groove changes across the loop, and it pushes FORWARD rather than filling.
  // A rim is a click with a pitch, so: triangle, huge penv, almost no decay.
  note("<~ ~ ~ ~ [~ ~ [~ c6] [~ c6]] [~ ~ [~ c6] [~ c6]] [~ ~ [~ c6] [~ c6]] [~ ~ [~ c6] [~ c6]]>")
    .s("triangle").penv(22).pdecay(0.006)
    .attack(0.0005).decay(0.022).sustain(0).gain(0.20).pan(0.62),

  // Turnarounds on beat 4 of bars 4 and 8, starting after the backbeat so the
  // run answers the clap instead of doubling it. Bar 8's is longer: it resets
  // the loop and needs to be heard as an ending, not a stumble.
  s("<~ ~ ~ [~ ~ ~ [~ ~ white white]] ~ ~ ~ [~ ~ [white white] [white white white white]]>")
    .hpf(1500).lpf(8000).attack(0.001).decay(0.055).sustain(0).gain(0.25),

  // Crash on the top of each half only. Noise with a long tail and a low-ish
  // hpf — a cymbal is broadband, so filtering it too high leaves a hiss.
  s("<white ~ ~ ~ white ~ ~ ~>")
    .hpf(3400).attack(0.002).decay(1.5).sustain(0)
    .gain(0.15).room(0.35).size(0.7)
)
